    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1312">

                <pb facs="1040" n="924" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg924"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.77/144.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President to the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cincinnati, Ohio</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-05-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 21, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have yours of May
                        17th<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1312-f1"/> relative to the presence of unemployed
                    railroad men along the Texas border. I approve and greatly appreciate the steps
                    you have taken to meet the situation.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wm. H. Taft.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Note.</hi>—Replies were received from the
                        railway presidents and receivers addressed by the Secretary of State on May
                        17; most of the letters were to the effect that the writer would be glad to
                        bear the matter in mind and had referred the Secretary’s request to the
                        appropriate official of his company.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1312-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1312-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; refers to above circular letter to railway presidents.</p>
            </note>
